    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Again the continuity of (ZˆK,ZˆI) induces the convergence of the two q functions. This completes the proof. Lemma 1.1: Z is closed in C [0,T];L2(I;R ⊗ R) equipped with norm · Z."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 21,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 53.999, 567.989, 558.103, 614.723 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Proof: First note Z is closed if and only if any convergent sequence (fn)∞n=1 in Z converges to some f ∈ Z. For the sake of contradiction, suppose there exists (fn)∞n=1 ∈ Z converges to f ∈/ Z. Then there exists t0 ∈ [0,T] such that there exists a measurable set I˜ ⊂ I with m(I˜) > 0, that f(t0)(x) > A¯+ǫ0 or f(t0)(x) < −A¯− ǫ0 for some ǫ0 > 0, x ∈ I˜. Here m is a measure equipped on I. Then ∀n ∈ N,"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 25,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 53.999, 523.188, 461.425, 557.912 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "|fn (t0)(x) − f (t0)(x)| dx > ǫ0 · m(I˜) There is contradiction and thus completes the proof."
  }, {
